1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
As a B.Tech Computer Science student, I spend a lot of time building projects.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:20,000
I’ve noticed the same thing among friends and fellow students: projects leave behind dependencies, build outputs, caches, and temporary files. Deleting the wrong thing can break a working project.

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:38,000
I built DevSweep AI to make that decision easier to review. Its guiding idea is simple: Clean. Keep. Restore. It identifies cleanup candidates, protects project files, and keeps a verified backup before an approved deletion.

4
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:48,000
I’m choosing the isolated synthetic demo workspace. This folder contains generated test data, not my application source.

5
00:00:48,000 --> 00:01:00,000
The scan found nine candidates with about 138 megabytes of candidate data. That number is an estimate of listed items, not storage already recovered.

6
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:12,000
For this run, the interface reports analysis by Nebius using NVIDIA Nemotron, and the live request succeeded.

7
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:25,000
The scanner supplies the candidate paths and measurements; the model adds recommendations. The backend still enforces the safety rules. Source and configuration are not cleanup candidates.

8
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:38,000
I’m reviewing the proposed plan and choosing just one small synthetic file: temp-artifact dot T-M-P, classified SAFE and measured at 96 kilobytes.

9
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:52,000
The plan shows exactly what will be removed. I review the path and risk, then explicitly approve execution in the application.

10
00:01:52,000 --> 00:02:04,000
The application reports that one item was deleted and zero failed. Its post-cleanup checks passed, including checks that the source directory and package manifest remain present.

11
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:15,000
The Git check was skipped because this synthetic fixture is not itself a Git repository.

12
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:25,000
Before deletion, DevSweep created a backup that passed SHA-256 verification. I reviewed the backup and confirmed the restore.

13
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:32,000
The application reports a completed restore without overwriting an existing file, and the restored test artifact matches its original hash.

14
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:45,000
I built DevSweep AI to help developers clean with confidence while keeping a recovery path. Clean. Keep. Restore. The project is on GitHub at Drishya-code slash DevSweep-AI.
